1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:02,880
I was a vet in Yorkshire.
那时，我在约克郡当兽医。

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:08,350
One day I received a call from Mrs Tompkin asking me to cut the beak of her budgie.
有一天，我接到汤普金太太打来的电话，请我给她的虎皮鹦鹉剪喙。

3
00:00:09,150 --> 00:00:15,650
I armed myself with a pair of clippers and stepped onto the narrow strip of pavement which separated the door from the road.
我带上一把剪子，踏上那条把房门和马路隔开的窄窄的人行道。

4
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:19,020
A pleasant-looking red-haired woman answered my knock.
应门的是一位红头发、面容和善的女士。

5
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:21,840
“I’m Mrs Dodds from next door,” she said.
“我是隔壁的多兹太太。”她说。

6
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:24,330
“I keep an eye on the old lady.
“我平时照看着这位老太太。

7
00:00:25,130 --> 00:00:26,960
She’s over eighty and lives alone.”
她八十多岁了，一个人住。”

8
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:29,860
She led me into the cramped little room.
她领我走进那间狭小的屋子。

9
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:35,700
“Here’s Mr Herriot coming to see Peter for you,” she said to the old woman who sat in a corner.
“哈利先生来给您看彼得了。”她对坐在角落里的老太太说。

10
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,580
Mrs Tompkin nodded and smiled, “Oh that’s good.
汤普金太太点点头，笑着说：“哦，那太好了。

11
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:44,410
Poor little fella can hardly eat with its long beak and I’m worried about him.
可怜的小家伙嘴巴那么长，几乎吃不了东西，我很担心它。

12
00:00:45,210 --> 00:00:47,670
He’s my only companion, you know.”
你知道，它是我唯一的伴儿。”

13
00:00:48,470 --> 00:00:50,990
“Yes, I understand, Mrs Tompkin.”
“是的，我明白，汤普金太太。”

14
00:00:51,790 --> 00:00:55,330
I looked at the cage by the window with the green budgie perched inside.
我看了看窗边的鸟笼，那只绿色的虎皮鹦鹉就栖在笼子里。

15
00:00:56,130 --> 00:00:59,720
“These little birds can be wonderful company when they start chattering.”
“这些小鸟一叽叽喳喳叫起来，可是很好的伴儿呢。”

16
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:01,300
She laughed.
她笑了。

17
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:03,630
“Yes, but it’s a funny thing.
“是啊，可说来也怪。

18
00:01:04,430 --> 00:01:06,170
Peter never has said that much.
彼得从来没怎么叫过。

19
00:01:06,970 --> 00:01:08,110
I think he’s lazy!
我看它是懒！

20
00:01:08,910 --> 00:01:10,570
But I just like having him with me.”
不过我就是喜欢有它陪着我。”

21
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:14,720
“Of course you do,” I said, “but he certainly needs attention now.”
“您当然喜欢，”我说，“不过它现在确实需要照料了。”

22
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:20,400
The beak was greatly overgrown, curving away down till it touched the feathers of the breast.
鸟喙长得太长了，一直向下弯，都碰到胸前的羽毛了。

23
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,380
I would be able to revolutionize his life with one quick snip from my clippers.
我只要用剪子麻利地一剪，就能彻底改变它的生活。

24
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:29,290
The way I was feeling, this job was right up my street.
照我当时的心情，这活儿正合我的胃口。

25
00:01:30,090 --> 00:01:33,050
I opened the cage door and slowly inserted my hand.
我打开笼门，慢慢地把手伸进去。

26
00:01:33,850 --> 00:01:35,870
“Come on, Peter,” I wheedled.
“来吧，彼得。”我哄着它说。

27
00:01:36,670 --> 00:01:41,040
As I lifted him out, I felt in my pocket with the other hand for the clippers.
我把它从笼子里捧出来，另一只手在口袋里摸剪子。

28
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:42,790
Then I stopped.
接着我愣住了。

29
00:01:43,590 --> 00:01:49,020
The tiny head was no longer poking cheekily from my fingers but had fallen loosely to one side.
那个小脑袋不再调皮地从我的指缝间探出来，而是软软地歪向了一边。

30
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:50,970
The eyes were closed.
它的眼睛闭上了。

31
00:01:51,770 --> 00:01:52,600
He was dead.
它死了。

32
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:55,790
Mrs Dodds and I looked at each other in horror.
我和多兹太太惊恐地对视了一眼。

33
00:01:56,590 --> 00:02:01,870
When I turned my head towards Mrs Tompkin, I was surprised to see that she was still nodding and smiling.
我转头看向汤普金太太，惊讶地发现她还在点头微笑。

34
00:02:02,670 --> 00:02:04,360
I drew her neighbor to one side.
我把她的邻居拉到一边。

35
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:07,560
“Mrs Dodds, how much does she see?”
“多兹太太，她能看清多少？”

36
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:11,960
“Oh, she’s very short-sighted but she’s right vain despite her age.
“哦，她眼睛高度近视，可她都这把年纪了，还特别爱面子。

37
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:14,380
Never would she wear glasses.
她说什么也不肯戴眼镜。

38
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:16,510
She’s hard of hearing, too.”
她耳朵也背。”

39
00:02:17,310 --> 00:02:18,370
“Well, look,” I said.
“好吧，你看。”我说。

40
00:02:19,170 --> 00:02:20,610
My heart was still pounding.
我的心还在怦怦直跳。

41
00:02:21,410 --> 00:02:22,840
“I just don’t know what to do.
“我真不知道该怎么办。

42
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:26,680
If I tell her about this, the shock will be terrible.
如果我把这事告诉她，她会受到可怕的打击。

43
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:28,650
Anything could happen.”
什么事都可能发生。”

44
00:02:29,450 --> 00:02:31,930
Mrs Dodds nodded, stricken-faced.
多兹太太点点头，一脸悲痛。

45
00:02:32,730 --> 00:02:33,720
“Yes, you’re right.
“是啊，你说得对。

46
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:36,250
She’s that attached to the little thing.”
她可疼那个小东西了。”

47
00:02:37,050 --> 00:02:40,470
I decided to do something for her instead of telling her the truth.
我决定为她做点什么，而不是把真相告诉她。

48
00:02:41,270 --> 00:02:46,420
It was a long time before I went to see how Mrs Tompkin got along with the bird I bought for her.
过了很久，我才去看汤普金太太和我给她买的那只鸟相处得怎么样。
